+    "**Reflections are special** because the difference between $\\text{E}(3)$ and $\\text{SE}(3)$ is exactly whether we also ask for invariance under them. Since $\\text{E}(3)$ additionally contains reflections, an $\\text{E}(3)$-invariant model assigns the same embedding to a molecule and its mirror image. That is a problem whenever the property we predict is *not* mirror-symmetric: two enantiomers of a chiral drug share all bond lengths and angles, yet can differ strongly in binding affinity and biological effect. For such targets, an $\\text{SE}(3)$-invariant model (rotations and translations only) is the right choice, since it can still tell left- from right-handed structures. The model we build in this talktorial operates on pairwise distances and is therefore $\\text{E}(3)$-invariant."
+   ]
+  },
